<blockquote>
Spotlight On...: The Last Word<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
Interview with <a href="mailto:eetum@tlwguild.org">Eetum</a> of <a href="http://www.tlwguild.org/">The Last Word</a><br />
<br />
The Spotlight On series of articles focuses on guilds which for one reason or another are of interest. You can expect a variety of guild types to be featured here, from "uber" to "casual" and from "rp" to "pvp", we'll try to feature as much variety as possible. In this inaugural issue we're taking a look at what is (arguably) the oldest Everquest II guild in 
existence. I'll be interviewing Eetum, Guild Leader of The Last Word.<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: Thank you for agreeing to this interview. Your guild was chosen for the first "Spotlight On" article because you've made the claim of being the oldest Everquest II guild in existence. When was The Last Word started, and by who?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: Thanks for this opportunity. In late 2000, after splitting off from another EverQuest guild in which I was second-in-command, I created The Last Word on the Tunare server. The phrase “the last word” is synonymous with paragons, role models, etc. TLW was a fun, casual, mid-level guild with occasional raids of places like Mistmoore, Najena, Sol A, and Crystal Caverns. Our priority was never loot, just to get out and have a good time with each other, and we did just that. We held many fun events, like a Freeport festival in February 2001, which included a scavenger hunt, arena roleplay event with prizes, trivia contest, and ample beer drinking. In early 2002, after an amazing year+ together, we decided to go our own separate ways. Soon after, I set my sights on EverQuest 2, which was scheduled to come out in over a year. Seeing that another guild was already prepared for EQ2 (the now-defunct Forces of Entropy), I decided that it was never too early to get organized. In August 2002 I created the EQ2 version of The Last Word. Since then we’ve switched our ezboard over to phpBB forums, on which we have over 17000 posts, and just last week we changed our HTML website over to the aesthetically pleasing PHP-Nuke. In the time before EQ2 release we’ve been biding our time by getting to know each other on the forums, formulating policies and strategies for release, and getting together in our IRC channel and games like EverQuest, Counter-Strike, Team Fortress Classic, WarCraft 3, and Yahoo Literati.<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: What type of guild is The Last Word? Do you fit into a "category"? How would you describe the guild itself?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: If I had to classify our guild, I’d say we are “semi-casual”. Our number one priority as a guild is not to get the best loot, become uber or anything like that; our goal is simply enjoyment of the game. As the reason people play games in the first place is to have fun, it seems logical for a guild to be dedicated to just that. We go and have gone about our goal a number of different ways – raiding, roleplaying, dueling, drinking, and more. Now because our priority is just having a good time, we aren’t all upset if a raid of ours goes awry. If we do a raid on Mistmoore, get halfway in and get clobbered because we’re in over our heads, we’ll retrieve the corpses, drink some beer, and head on over to the Freeport arena for some good ol’ drunken brawling. Because of our light atmosphere and focus on enjoying ourselves, we can declare that night a success (since we had a good time) where other guilds would declare it a failure (since we got mostly wiped out and didn’t clear the zone). Now of course our guild isn’t for everyone, but the people who are in TLW have a great time. Based on all the feedback we’ve gotten from members of the EQ1 version of TLW, who say how they’ve had more fun in our guild than any they’ve ever been in, we know we’re doing something right.<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: How many members do you currently have waiting to join the guild when it goes live?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: At the moment we have 40 members on our roster. The number of people in our guild has gone up and down quite a bit over the months, as we are in a continual process of accepting people who show promise, and cutting from the roster those who either don’t exhibit the qualities we expect of a TLW member, or who don’t give enough time to the guild to show that they are really interested. I suspect we’ll have a tight group of active members, probably somewhere between 30 and 40, once we get on our feet after release.<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: Could you explain what kind of governmental system your guild has? How many officers are there, and what are their roles?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: At the moment we have four officers, all of which were also officers in the EQ1 version of TLW. We are holding off assigning roles like treasurer and raid coordinator until after release, so we can get a good judge of exactly what is needed and who would be best suited for the job. Our only formal role at the moment is Guild Diplomat, a position currently held by my officer Fonen. As far as a governmental system goes, I’d say ours might be called a democratic autocracy. All the big decisions, like creation of guild policy, applicant admission, etc., are made by myself, occasionally with input from my officers. If I ever feel that a decision of mine would be ill-received, I’ll ask for the opinions of the whole guild. We try to keep things simple, so less attention needs to be given to governing the guild, and more time spent actually enjoying the game. While I do encourage the people to come to me if they have an idea to improve our guild, I see no reason to ask the members of TLW if they consent to every little policy change or minor thing I want to implement. Our simple system of operation worked wonders in the first EverQuest, and it is proving to be just as effective in the lead-up to the release of EQ2. <br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: What region of the world do most of your players come from?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: Nearly all of our guildmembers reside in the United States. We have a few military guys, some of which are currently stationed in Europe and the Middle East. A look at the time zone column of our roster shows that more TLW members live on the U.S. East Coast than anywhere else. However, as high-end raiding is not our guild’s priority, we gladly accept people from any time zone around the world. <br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: Is there a specific server (type) the guild plans to settle on?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: We will be forming on a blue (PvE) server. If Tunare is available as a blue server at release, we will form there.<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: What plans does the guild have for post-release?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: I could tell you, but then I’d have to eet you. =) One of our biggest priorities in the time shortly after release is being one of the first guilds to obtain a guildhall. As we will almost definitely be the first cohesive, highly-organized guild on our server, this should prove to be an easily achievable goal. Exactly what shape the guild will take in the long run, like whether we lean more towards raiding, roleplaying, or anything else as a means to achieve our goal of having fun, ultimately depends on the opinions and playing styles of the TLW members themselves. <br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Mayhem: Thank you for taking the time to complete this interview. Do you have any last words for the readers of CoG?</span><br />
<br />
Eetum: I’d like to extend an invitation to anyone interested to check out <a href="http://www.tlwguild.org/">our website</a> and chat with us on <a href="http://www.tlwguild.org/forum/">our forum</a>. If you ever need technical advice or want someone to talk to, <a href="mailto:eetum@tlwguild.org">my inbox</a> is open. See you all in EverQuest 2! (Well, some of you anyway ;D )<br />
<br />
<span class="sig">Interviewee Details:</span><br />
Guild: The Last Word<br />
Guild Leader: Eetum<br />
